hypotheses.statement:
There is an absolute constant $C<\infty$ such that, for every integer $k\ge2$, every metric space $(X,d)$ with at least $k+1$ points, and every $s\in X^k$, there exist a randomized online policy $A$ and a finite $B\ge0$ satisfying $$\label{eq:main}
 \mathbb E\,\mathop{\mathrm{cost}}_{A,s}(\sigma)
 \le C\bigl(\log(k+1)\bigr)^2\mathop{\mathrm{OPT}}_{X,d,k,s}(\sigma)+B$$ for every finite request sequence $\sigma$.

For every internal vertex $v$ and every allowed $\beta$, $$\label{eq:rank-superadditivity}
 \sum_{i\prec v}L_i^\beta\le L_v^\beta,\qquad
 \sum_{i\prec v}M_i\le M_v\le m_v.$$

Given a sufficiently small absolute $\epsilon_b>0$, the remaining absolute tolerances can be chosen so that there are caps $d_i=n_i+b_i$, zero on inactive children, with $$\label{eq:cap-feasibility}
 \begin{aligned}
 f_i^{\beta_i}\le b_i\le f_i^{\beta_v}
                                      &&(i\text{ regular}),\\
 T:=f_o^{\beta_v}\le b_o\le(1+\epsilon_b/\ell)T,
 \qquad c_DT\le D\le C_DT
                                      &&(o\text{ dominant}).
 \end{aligned}$$ Here $c_D,C_D>0$ are absolute, fixed independently of $\epsilon_b$ and the fine tolerances; in particular $D=0$ if and only if $T=0$.

There is a causal update schedule satisfying <a href="#eq:alpha-feasibility" data-reference-type="eqref" data-reference="eq:alpha-feasibility">[eq:alpha-feasibility]</a> and <a href="#eq:side-feasibility" data-reference-type="eqref" data-reference="eq:side-feasibility">[eq:side-feasibility]</a> after every step, such that $$\label{eq:proportion-movement}
 \mathbb E\sum_{t\text{ not wholesale}}
   \left(S_t\|\alpha_t-\alpha_{t-1}\|_1
        +D_t\|w_t-w_{t-1}\|_1\right)
       \le C\ell E_v+D'_v.$$

If $R_{t,j,\lambda}$ denotes a refresh, then $$\label{eq:key-size-refresh-budget}
 \mathbb E\sum_{t,j,\lambda\in\Lambda_j^H}
 r_j\bigl(\widehat m_{t-1,j,\lambda}
            +\widehat m_{t,j,\lambda}\bigr)\mathbf 1_{R_{t,j,\lambda}}
 \le C\ell P+D_{\rm size},$$ where $C$ is absolute and $D_{\rm size}$ depends only on the fixed metric, scales, $k$, and label pools.

outputs.exact_statement:
There is an absolute constant $C<\infty$ such that, for every integer $k\ge2$, every metric space $(X,d)$ with at least $k+1$ points, and every $s\in X^k$, there exist a randomized online policy $A$ and a finite $B\ge0$ satisfying $$\label{eq:main}
 \mathbb E\,\mathop{\mathrm{cost}}_{A,s}(\sigma)
 \le C\bigl(\log(k+1)\bigr)^2\mathop{\mathrm{OPT}}_{X,d,k,s}(\sigma)+B$$ for every finite request sequence $\sigma$. The constant $C$ is independent of every property of the metric. The number $B$ may depend on $(X,d),k,s$, but not on the sequence or its length. The same policy serves all finite sequences and horizons. If the initial positions are distinct, one may take $B=0$.

There are absolute constants $C_0,C_1$ and $\tau_0$ with the following property. For $\tau\ge\tau_0$, the observed histories determine amounts $q_v(t)$ satisfying $$\label{eq:allocation-constraints}
 q_{\mathrm{root}}=k,\qquad
 0\le q_v\le C_0m_v,\qquad
 \sum_{i\prec v}q_i\le q_v,\qquad q_{x_t}\ge1.$$ Moreover, with $\ell=1+\log(k+1)$, $$\label{eq:allocation-bound}
 V:=\mathbb E\sum_{t\ge1}\sum_{v\ne\mathrm{root}}
       r_{|v|-1}|q_v(t)-q_v(t-1)|
 \le C_1\ell P+D_{\mathcal T}.$$ Here sums stop at the chosen finite horizon. The finite constant $D_{\mathcal T}$ depends only on the fixed tree, radii, and $k$; it can be chosen independently of the input law, horizon, and number of histories. No initial allocation is prescribed.

For every internal vertex $v$ and every allowed $\beta$, $$\label{eq:rank-superadditivity}
 \sum_{i\prec v}L_i^\beta\le L_v^\beta,\qquad
 \sum_{i\prec v}M_i\le M_v\le m_v.$$ No independence between child counts is required.

The data above satisfy, for an absolute constant $C$ and a finite $D_v^{\mathrm{data}}$ depending only on the fixed star and $k$, $$\label{eq:coarse-data-budget}
             \mathbb E\sum_t C_{v,t}\le C E_v+D_v^{\mathrm{data}}.$$ The constant is uniform in the law and horizon. Every active child obeys $$\label{eq:parameter-gap}
        0\le h_i\le C\ell,\qquad
        \beta_i-\beta_v\ge\frac{c_g h_i}{\ell}$$ for an absolute $c_g>0$. If a dominant index and sides are present, then $0<u<1/4$, and with $H=1+\log(1/u)$ every side satisfies $h_i\ge cH$ for an absolute $c>0$. In addition, $n_i+B_i+\sup_\beta f_i^\beta\le C a_i$ at active children, and all these quantities vanish at inactive children.

Given a sufficiently small absolute $\epsilon_b>0$, the remaining absolute tolerances can be chosen so that there are caps $d_i=n_i+b_i$, zero on inactive children, with $$\label{eq:cap-feasibility}
 \begin{aligned}
 f_i^{\beta_i}\le b_i\le f_i^{\beta_v}
                                      &&(i\text{ regular}),\\
 T:=f_o^{\beta_v}\le b_o\le(1+\epsilon_b/\ell)T,
 \qquad c_DT\le D\le C_DT
                                      &&(o\text{ dominant}).
 \end{aligned}$$ Here $c_D,C_D>0$ are absolute, fixed independently of $\epsilon_b$ and the fine tolerances; in particular $D=0$ if and only if $T=0$. At every child $d_i\le C M_i\le C m_i$. Their variation away from wholesale changes satisfies $$\label{eq:fine-cap-budget}
 \mathbb E\sum_{t\notin\mathcal W_v}\sum_i|d_i(t)-d_i(t-1)|
          \le C\ell E_v+D_v^{\mathrm{cap}},$$ where $D_v^{\mathrm{cap}}$ is uniform in the law and horizon. At wholesale changes the old plus new caps are bounded by $C$ times the old plus new active totals.

Choose $C_\alpha$ sufficiently large after fixing $c_t$. For any old vector $\alpha^0$ in the same simplex, a global minimizer $$\label{eq:alpha-minimization}
 \alpha^1\in\mathop{\mathrm{arg\,min}}_\alpha
       \left\{G^\alpha(\alpha;B)+S\|\alpha-\alpha^0\|_1\right\}$$ satisfies <a href="#eq:alpha-feasibility" data-reference-type="eqref" data-reference="eq:alpha-feasibility">[eq:alpha-feasibility]</a> and $$\label{eq:alpha-update-payment}
 S\|\alpha^1-\alpha^0\|_1
       \le G^\alpha(\alpha^0;B)-G^\alpha(\alpha^1;B).$$ The same assertions hold with the automatic singleton rule.

Choose the absolute multiplier $C'_w$ sufficiently large after fixing the small constants. For every old vector $w^0$ in the side domain, a global minimizer $$\label{eq:side-minimization}
 w^1\in\mathop{\mathrm{arg\,min}}_w\left\{G^w(w;B,D)+D\|w-w^0\|_1\right\}$$ satisfies <a href="#eq:side-feasibility" data-reference-type="eqref" data-reference="eq:side-feasibility">[eq:side-feasibility]</a>, and $$\label{eq:side-update-payment}
 D\|w^1-w^0\|_1\le G^w(w^0;B,D)-G^w(w^1;B,D).$$ This holds for arbitrary nonnegative $B,D$ with $S=\sum_iB_i$, without a requirement that they arise from ranks.

There is a causal update schedule satisfying <a href="#eq:alpha-feasibility" data-reference-type="eqref" data-reference="eq:alpha-feasibility">[eq:alpha-feasibility]</a> and <a href="#eq:side-feasibility" data-reference-type="eqref" data-reference="eq:side-feasibility">[eq:side-feasibility]</a> after every step, such that $$\label{eq:proportion-movement}
 \mathbb E\sum_{t\text{ not wholesale}}
   \left(S_t\|\alpha_t-\alpha_{t-1}\|_1
        +D_t\|w_t-w_{t-1}\|_1\right)
       \le C\ell E_v+D'_v.$$ Vectors are extended by zero to the fixed child set. The side term is zero without a dominant child. The simplex term includes the automatic singleton state; an empty active domain contributes zero. The finite constant $D'_v$ is uniform in the law and horizon.

Fix a template and constants as in <a href="#eq:pilot-concentration-constants" data-reference-type="eqref" data-reference="eq:pilot-concentration-constants">[eq:pilot-concentration-constants]</a>. At each radius let $g_j$ be any function with $0\le g_j\le1$ and $r_j\mathop{\mathrm{Lip}}(g_j)\le L$. For measures satisfying <a href="#eq:pilot-mover" data-reference-type="eqref" data-reference="eq:pilot-mover">[eq:pilot-mover]</a>, $$\sum_{j=1}^J
 [\mathcal L_{r_j}(\mu^+,g_j)
        -\mathcal L_{r_j}(\mu^-,g_j)]_+
 \le C_{\mathrm{dr}}\ell e_x.
 \label{eq:pilot-drift}$$ The constant is independent of $Y$, $k$, $J$, and $\tau\ge2$. For example, set $$\begin{aligned}
 p_0&=\left\lceil\log_2(D_1/\gamma)\right\rceil,\nonumber\\
 C_{\mathrm{dr}}
   &=\frac{2056}{\gamma}+\frac{8224}{\sigma}
       +\frac{2\Lambda p_0}{\delta\log2}
       +2C_{\mathrm{loc}}
                   \left(\frac{p_0}{\log2}+2\gamma\right).
 \label{eq:pilot-drift-constant}
\end{aligned}$$

If $R_{t,j,\lambda}$ denotes a refresh, then $$\label{eq:key-size-refresh-budget}
 \mathbb E\sum_{t,j,\lambda\in\Lambda_j^H}
 r_j\bigl(\widehat m_{t-1,j,\lambda}
            +\widehat m_{t,j,\lambda}\bigr)\mathbf 1_{R_{t,j,\lambda}}
 \le C\ell P+D_{\rm size},$$ where $C$ is absolute and $D_{\rm size}$ depends only on the fixed metric, scales, $k$, and label pools.

Fix any completed fractional state and any $y\in Y$. At each level whose heavy-center set has a center within $7r_j$ of $y$, let $\lambda_j$ be its label. Such a center is unique. Then $$\label{eq:pointwise-park-coefficients}
 \sum_{\substack{j:\ d(y,a_{j,\lambda_j})\le7r_j}}
                \frac{b_{j,\lambda_j}}{\widehat m_{j,\lambda_j}}
 \le C\ell,$$ with an absolute $C$, independent of the state and the number of levels.

Assume the fractional allocations and anchors constructed in the preceding sections, with $\tau\ge22$. They admit a causal rounding satisfying <a href="#eq:rounding-invariants" data-reference-type="eqref" data-reference="eq:rounding-invariants">[eq:rounding-invariants]</a> for which $$\label{eq:rounding-global}
 N_{\rm rnd}:=\mathbb E\sum_{t=1}^H\sum_{v\ne{\rm root}}
                 r_{|v|-1}|n_v(t)-n_v(t-1)|\le132V.$$ Placing the integer parks at their current anchors gives simulated configurations $S_t$ of exactly $k$ members containing each current request. There is a matching of consecutive configurations with expected total cost at most $$\label{eq:rounding-realization}
                         120N_{\rm rnd}+40T_{\rm anchor}.$$ Consequently their expected movement is at most $C(V+\ell P+\ell Q)+D$ for an absolute $C$ and a finite additive term $D$ depending only on the fixed finite structure.
